The congestion control a hysteria client actually sends with, which is not the one ngtcp2 ships.

`UseBBR` does not configure quic-go's congestion control — it REPLACES it. Every hysteria client
that is given no bandwidth in its configuration calls
`conn.SetCongestionControl(bbr.NewBbrSender(...))`
(vendor/hysteria/core/internal/congestion/utils.go:37-43, reached from core/client/client.go:153),
and what that installs is the package hysteria carries in core/internal/congestion/bbr: a port of
Chromium's BBRv1, about 2600 lines of Go. ngtcp2's `NGTCP2_CC_ALGO_BBR` is BBRv2 — a different
state machine, different gains, a different bandwidth estimator, a different response to loss. So
this client, which matched the reference byte for byte in its packets, was sending them to a rhythm
no reference client has: the one thing on this plane a path sees without decrypting anything.

This patch adds that algorithm as `NGTCP2_CC_ALGO_HYBBR` in lib/ngtcp2_hybbr.{c,h}, ported from:

  core/internal/congestion/bbr/bbr_sender.go               the state machine, the gains, the cycle, recovery, the windows
  core/internal/congestion/bbr/bandwidth_sampler.go        per-packet sampling, the ack-aggregation tracker, A_0 selection
  core/internal/congestion/bbr/windowed_filter.go          Kathleen Nichols' three-estimate filter, for bandwidth and ack height
  core/internal/congestion/bbr/packet_number_indexed_queue.go, core/internal/congestion/bbr/ringbuffer.go   what the sampler keeps sent packets in
  core/internal/congestion/bbr/bandwidth.go                the arithmetic, in its order of operations
  core/internal/congestion/common/pacer.go          the token bucket, for the two numbers ngtcp2 needs from it

Every constant is the reference's and every function carries the line it came from. The reference has
three profiles (standard, conservative, aggressive) and their switches are all implemented and
honoured here — drain-to-target, overshoot detection, ack aggregation during startup, overestimate
avoidance, the reduced extra-acked on a bandwidth increase. What is NOT here is a way to select
anything but standard, because nothing in this client's configuration selects one: hysteria's own
default is the empty profile, which `configForProfile` answers with standard.

HOW THE PACE IS APPLIED — and this is no longer a difference. The reference hands its rate to a
token bucket that quic-go's send loop consults before every packet: send while there is budget, stop
when there is not, wait no less than MinPacingDelay (core/internal/congestion/common/pacer.go:32-76). That bucket is ported
here too, and since 19 September this client's send loop asks it the same way — see the note above
the write loop in src/quic/conn.c. ngtcp2's own pacing is left unused: `ngtcp2_conn_update_pkt_tx_time`
is never called, so the library never holds anything back itself and the only thing pacing this
connection is the reference's bucket, filled at the reference's rate.

The first version of this patch paced ngtcp2's way instead — write a batch, then wait that batch's
worth of time — with the rate and the burst ceiling taken from the bucket. The relay in
test/hystand/loss.sh, which reports the shape each client arrives in, said that was not enough: the
reference's datagrams arrived back-to-back nine times in ten against eight in ten here. Asking the
bucket per packet did not close that either, and the batch size turned out to be the reason (see
LC_GSO_BATCH). What remains of it is measured and named there.


Two smaller seams, both stated where they are crossed:

  - ngtcp2 reports a packet to a controller BEFORE adding it to the in-flight count, and the
    reference is told the count WITH the packet already in it (internal/ackhandler/sent_packet_handler.go:317-325), so
    the packet is added at the call;
  - ngtcp2 reports only ack-eliciting packets. The reference also sees ACK-only ones, where it
    moves nothing but `lastSentPacket` and, when the connection was idle, `exitingQuiescence`.

The batching is exact rather than approximate: ngtcp2 removes every acknowledged packet from the
in-flight count before the first `on_pkt_acked`, detects loss next, and calls `on_ack_recv` last
(ngtcp2_rtb.c, rtb_recv_ack), so an ack's acknowledged and lost packets are collected as they are
reported and the reference's OnCongestionEventEx runs once, at the end, with the in-flight this ack
started with recovered exactly.

